Tests only: no installed file is touched.

The last check of the two serialize tests feeds unserialize() a payload in
the legacy "C:" format, which ext-decimal 2.x does not read, and expects
PHP's answer to it: a warning and an empty object. Since PHP 8.6,
unserialize() fails for such a payload of a class without an unserializer
(warning, then false): php/php-src#22058, the fix for GH-22046, where such
an empty object of an internal class could crash PHP. php/php-src#24020
kept the empty instance for __PHP_Incomplete_Class only. The code is
object_custom() in ext/standard/var_unserializer.re. Expect the new answer.
The checks of the extension's own format (serialize() and the round trip)
are unchanged.

Not sent upstream: the expectation holds for PHP 8.6 and later only. Drop
once upstream adapts the two tests.

diff --git a/tests/Decimal/serialize.phpt b/tests/Decimal/serialize.phpt
index 9447351..6c0c23f 100644
--- a/tests/Decimal/serialize.phpt
+++ b/tests/Decimal/serialize.phpt
@@ -21,5 +21,6 @@ object(Decimal\Decimal)#%d (2) {
 }
 
 Warning: Class Decimal\Decimal has no unserializer in %s on line %d
-object(Decimal\Decimal)#%d (0) {
-}
+
+Warning: unserialize(): Error at offset 27 of 54 bytes in %s on line %d
+bool(false)
